---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Reading from '../../../layouts/Reading.astro';

import landscaping from '../../../assets/portrait/landscaping.jpg';

// The side column is 24rem (384px) at full page width.
const sideWidths = [384, 768];
const sideSizes = '(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 384px';

/*
  One manual project a year. The two named below are the two the about page
  names — the wall and the orchard. Everything else is a placeholder, because
  a list of jobs you have not done is the fastest way to make a page about
  physical work read as fiction.

  Add a year, and add a photograph with it. This is the one section of the
  site where a paragraph without a picture is genuinely worse than no
  paragraph: the whole claim is that the thing exists in the world.
*/
const projects = [
  {
    year: 'This year',
    title: 'A small orchard',
    note: 'The interesting part of it is four years away. TODO — how many trees, which varieties, and why those.',
  },
  {
    year: 'Last year',
    title: 'A dry-stone wall',
    note: 'No mortar, so every stone has to earn its place twice — once for the face and once for the course behind it. TODO — length, how long it took, and what you would do differently.',
  },
  {
    year: 'TODO',
    title: 'TODO — an earlier project',
    note: 'TODO — the rule is one a year, so there are more of these. Fill them in backwards until you run out of ones you remember properly.',
  },
];
---
<Reading
  title="Gardening & Landscaping — Kevin Holler"
  description="One manual project a year: the walls, beds, and built work in Kevin Holler's garden in Würzburg."
  here="garden"
  layout="spread"
>
  <div class="spread">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <h1>Gardening &amp; landscaping</h1>
      <p class="stamp">One manual project a year, on purpose.</p>

      <p class="lede">
        Every year I take on a project that has nothing to do with screens.
        Last year it was a dry-stone wall. This year, a small orchard.
      </p>

      <p>
        The rule is deliberate and the reasoning is not complicated. Software
        is finished in the sense that it stops changing when you stop touching
        it, and it is never finished in any other sense. A wall is finished.
        You stand back from it, and it is either standing or it is not, and no
        amount of arguing about it in a document changes which.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is also the only work I do where the mistakes are permanent and
        cheap at the same time — the worst outcome is a season lost and a
        weekend spent taking something apart, which is a rate of learning I
        would take in any discipline.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="spread-side">
      <figure class="figure">
        <Image
          src={landscaping}
          alt="Garden landscaping project — playhouse and bench."
          widths={sideWidths}
          sizes={sideSizes}
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
        <figcaption>The garden · Würzburg</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="spread">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <h2>The projects</h2>
      <p>
        Newest first. What is planted rather than built is on
        <a href="/new/food-and-wine/">food &amp; wine</a>, since most of it
        ends up in the kitchen anyway.
      </p>

      <ul class="entries" style="margin-top: 20px;">
        {
          projects.map((project) => (
            <li>
              <div class="row">
                <span class="name">{project.title}</span>
                <span class="years">{project.year}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="blurb">{project.note}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2>What I have learned doing it</h2>
      <p>
        TODO — three or four paragraphs. This is the section that makes the
        page worth reading rather than worth glancing at, and it is the one
        nobody else can write. Where the manual work has changed how you think
        about the other work is the obvious thread, but only if it is actually
        true rather than a neat ending.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="spread-side"></div>
  </div>
</Reading>
